win32u: Report a DPI-scaled window's flush as failed when its target surface refused it.

A driver flush returns FALSE to mean "not yet: keep the bounds dirty and flush again" (winewayland does
this until a window's first xdg configure). For a DPI-scaled window that FALSE landed on the target
surface, which win32u never flushes by itself (only the wrapper is registered), while the wrapper
reported TRUE and dropped its own bounds. A window that painted before its configure and then waited
in GetMessage was never flushed again and never appeared. An installer's MessageBox is exactly that:
at 175% a DPI-unaware OK/Cancel box never showed, so the installer waited forever for an answer.

The wrapper now returns FALSE while the target still holds dirty bounds, so the next pass stretches
and flushes again, as it does for a window without a wrapper.

--- a/dlls/win32u/dce.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
@@ -216,7 +216,23 @@
     }
 
     window_surface_flush( surface->target_surface );
-    return TRUE;
+
+    /* NEUTRON: report the target's refusal. A driver flush returns FALSE to say "not yet, keep the bounds
+     * dirty and flush again" (winewayland: the window has no xdg configure yet). For a DPI-scaled window
+     * that FALSE landed on the target surface, which win32u never flushes by itself (only this wrapper is
+     * registered), while this wrapper reported TRUE and dropped its own bounds. An application that painted
+     * before the configure and then waited in GetMessage was never flushed again, so its window never
+     * appeared: measured with a DPI-unaware test window at 175% (never shown, 2/2) against the same window
+     * per-monitor aware (shown, 2/2). Returning FALSE keeps this wrapper's bounds dirty, so the next pass
+     * stretches and flushes again, as it does for a window without a wrapper. */
+    {
+        BOOL flushed;
+
+        window_surface_lock( surface->target_surface );
+        flushed = IsRectEmpty( &surface->target_surface->bounds );
+        window_surface_unlock( surface->target_surface );
+        return flushed;
+    }
 }
 
 static void scaled_surface_destroy( struct window_surface *window_surface )
